mogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =6\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+2.\n$$" 13194139534647 true] [17592186229593 1145 "Try the constant particular solution $y_p=B$. The equation gives $3B=6$, so $B=2$. Adding the complementary solution yields $y(x)=Ae^{-3x}+2$, with arbitrary constant $A$." 13194139534647 false] [17592186229597 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7y=3x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534647 true] [17592186229597 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } + 7y = 3x + 4$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534647 false] [17592186229597 1145 "The complementary solution solves the homogeneous equation $y_c^{\\prime}+7y_c=0$. Separating a nonzero solution gives $dy_c/y_c=-7\\,dx$, so $y_c=Ae^{-7x}$. Allowing $A=0$ also includes the zero solution." 13194139534647 false] [17592186229597 1145 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-7x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-7x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534647 true] [17592186301206 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301207 13194139534647 true] [17592186301206 1089 "q-331877" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534647 true] [17592186301206 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=3{x}^{2}-2x+5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534647 true] [17592186301206 1113 #uuid "279c6e70-99fa-5736-91b3-186d8e98a23c" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301206 1145 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301208 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301209 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301210 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301211 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301212 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1041 "selection" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301208 13194139534647 true] [17592186301207 1110 #uuid "a954b816-d048-57b5-afe1-dd002c9cd01f" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301208 1034 "A{e}^{4x}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301208 1111 #uuid "2015e534-fba6-51c1-a9d5-9ae2b3892120" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301209 1034 "A{e}^{-2x}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301209 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301209 1111 #uuid "ce55c6ac-cee0-5916-b7ad-2ef46a12a4de" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301210 1034 "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301210 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301210 1111 #uuid "73770f48-2dfb-5828-95b6-c6f72b8882dd" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301211 1034 "A{e}^{-4x}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301211 1111 #uuid "be564220-7731-5480-b9e5-fa73ceef3eb6" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301212 1034 "A{e}^{2x}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301212 1111 #uuid "1c58247e-7981-56da-8b5e-47ae4ce2630a" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301213 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301214 13194139534647 true] [17592186301213 1089 "q-331920" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301213 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534647 true] [17592186301213 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{x}^{2}-4x+1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534647 true] [17592186301213 1113 #uuid "d9cadaa1-0348-5108-b0ed-d95da25f0147" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301213 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301215 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301216 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301217 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301218 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301219 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1041 "selection" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301215 13194139534647 true] [17592186301214 1110 #uuid "ec8a9c39-5604-5b2d-b090-9fee7d8b9984" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301215 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301215 1111 #uuid "55ab4aa1-0dbe-52d4-8331-29491c0009fa" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301216 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-4x+1" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301216 1111 #uuid "b3234d59-b988-5b25-98fc-55cbdac73e55" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301217 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}-4x+4" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301217 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139