194139534647 true] [17592186301273 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534647 true] [17592186301273 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301273 1113 #uuid "23d16a30-c940-5f1b-8608-970838c99c44" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301273 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-6\\beta =12\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =0 \\\\ A-2 & =0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\,{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301274 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301275 13194139534647 true] [17592186301274 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534647 true] [17592186301274 1041 "field-1" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301274 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301275 13194139534647 true] [17592186301274 1110 #uuid "de2350d3-6b2c-559b-a260-10a5fa8d1783" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301275 1034 "2e^{6x}-2" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301275 1111 #uuid "351a888f-ff0d-5b36-9dad-1269415b1d0c" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301277 13194139534647 true] [17592186301276 1089 "q-332988" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301276 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534647 true] [17592186301276 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{x}^{2}+3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." 13194139534647 true] [17592186301276 1113 #uuid "17577237-e9e4-56d2-b8e7-b2f014655c05" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301276 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=2{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(\\beta -2\\gamma )=2{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =0\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -2\\gamma =3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2(0)}-(0)^{2}-(0)-2 & =1 \\\\ A-2 & =1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301278 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301279 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301280 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301281 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301282 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1041 "selection" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301280 13194139534647 true] [17592186301277 1110 #uuid "eccc5358-a2cf-58