g the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =-9.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x-9.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2x-9.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2(0)-9 & =3 \\\\ A-9 & =3 \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=12\\,{e}^{x}-2x-9.\n$$" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301292 13194139534647 true] [17592186301291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534647 true] [17592186301291 1041 "field-1" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301292 13194139534647 true] [17592186301291 1110 #uuid "8d87bf10-e58d-506c-960b-9ab113128e61" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301292 1034 "12e^{x}-2x-9" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301292 1111 #uuid "05ef04e4-5980-59f8-b00a-478ee63bb877" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301293 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301294 13194139534647 true] [17592186301293 1089 "q-333057" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301293 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534647 true] [17592186301293 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4,\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301293 1113 #uuid "693b99ff-648b-58e8-8cc1-0950d6dd7372" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301293 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\beta =4\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =5 \\\\ A-2 & =5 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301294 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301295 13194139534647 true] [17592186301294 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301296 13194139534647 true] [17592186301294 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534647 true] [17592186301294 1041 "field-1" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301294 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301296 13194139534647 true] [17592186301294 1110 #uuid "f5ec826e-d1b6-518c-8292-d0f81ca1b2e9" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301295 1034 "7e^{2x}+2" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534647 true] [17592186301295 1111 #uuid "cc5497ea-33d0-5f4b-877d-4acdeac8c443" 13194139534647 true] [17592186301296 1034 "7e^{